  "result": "Japan and Korea customers should now be charged the invoice total: a ¥1,500 invoice produces a charge of 1500. `npm test` passes, 8 of 8, including the new tests.\n\n**Root cause:** `buildChargePayload` in `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lower-cases the currency code for the gateway (`jpy`). It then passed that lower-case code to `toMinor`. The currency registry in `src/lib/currency/registry.js` is keyed by upper-case code, so `exponent('jpy')` found no match and fell back to 2 decimals. That multiplied zero-decimal currencies by 100. USD and EUR use 2 decimals, so the fallback gave the right answer and hid the bug. KWD and BHD, which use 3 decimals, were affected the same way (10× too low).\n\n**Fix:** the payload now upper-cases the code before the minor-unit conversion. The code sent to the gateway is still lower-case.\n\n**Tests:** I added three regression tests in `test/charge.test.js`. JPY 1500 and KRW 20000 are charged as-is. KWD 1.5 is charged as 1500.\n\nI didn't touch the registry's silent fallback, so an unrecognised currency code can still be charged at 2 decimals. Making `exponent()` throw on unknown codes would stop this class of bug from recurring quietly. I left it alone because it could break other callers (refunds and payouts) that I haven't checked.",
